    pr = round(100 * overview[c]['room_type_counts']['Private room'] / overview[c]['total_listings'], 1)
    html += f"""
      <tr>
        <td>{c}</td>
        <td>{v['total']:,}</td>
        <td>{v['professional_listings']:,}</td>
        <td><strong>{v['professional_share']}%</strong></td>
        <td>{eh}%</td>
        <td>{pr}%</td>
      </tr>"""

html += """
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Professional dominance varies dramatically.</strong> Bangkok (60.4%) and Barcelona (58.3%) have the highest professional host concentration — a majority of their listings are managed by operators with 5+ properties. Amsterdam stands out at the opposite extreme with just 6.8% professional share, suggesting strong regulatory enforcement or a genuinely peer-driven market. London and Rome occupy the middle ground (~30%), while Paris (21.4%) and Sydney (42.3%) bracket the median range. High professional share combined with high Entire Home share (as in Bangkok and Barcelona) signals advanced market commercialization.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: GUEST DEMAND -->
<section id="guest-demand">
  <h2>4. Guest Demand & Review Activity</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Using review metrics as demand proxies: frequency (reviews per month), recency (last 12 months), and market engagement (% of listings with any reviews).</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    """ + rpm_svg + """
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="text-align:left">City</th>
        <th>Avg Reviews/Month</th>
        <th>Median Reviews/Month</th>
        <th>Avg Reviews (LTM)</th>
        <th>Avg Total Reviews</th>
        <th>% With Any Reviews</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>"""

for c, v in rpm_sorted:
    html += f"""
      <tr>
        <td>{c}</td>
        <td><strong>{v['avg_reviews_per_month']}</strong></td>
        <td>{v['median_reviews_per_month']}</td>
        <td>{v['avg_reviews_ltm']}</td>
        <td>{v['avg_total_reviews']}</td>
        <td>{v['pct_with_reviews']}%</td>
      </tr>"""

html += """
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Rome leads in guest engagement</strong> with the highest average reviews per month (1.77) and median (1.28), plus the strongest last-12-month activity (14.35 avg reviews LTM). This suggests Rome's Airbnb market has particularly high booking velocity relative to its supply. Sydney and Barcelona follow with above-average engagement. Bangkok has the lowest review penetration (only 64.4% of listings have any reviews), indicating a significant share of inactive or newly listed properties.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: AVAILABILITY & BOOKING CONSTRAINTS -->
<section id="availability-minimumnights">
  <h2>5. Availability & Booking Constraints</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">How accessible are listings to travelers? Availability signals commercial intent; minimum-night requirements reveal regulatory fingerprints.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-container">
      """ + avail_svg + """
    </div>
    <div class="chart-container">
      """ + mn30_svg + """
    </div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="text-align:left">City</th>
        <th>Mean Availability (days)</th>
        <th>Median Availability</th>
        <th>Mean Min Nights</th>
        <th>Median Min Nights</th>
        <th>% with 30+ Min Nights</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>"""

for c, v in sorted(avail_data.items(), key=lambda x: x[1]['mean_availability'], reverse=True):
    html += f"""
      <tr>
        <td>{c}</td>
        <td>{v['mean_availability']}</td>
        <td>{int(v['median_availability'])}</td>
        <td>{v['mean_min_nights']}</td>
        <td>{int(v['median_min_nights'])}</td>
        <td><strong>{v['pct_30plus_min_nights']}%</strong></td>
      </tr>"""

html += """
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Regulatory signatures are visible in minimum-night data.</strong> Barcelona stands out: 40.1% of listings require 30+ night stays, strongly suggesting local regulations that restrict short-term rentals. Paris follows at 20.4%, consistent with its well-documented short-term rental cap (typically 120 days/year for primary residences, pushing many hosts toward longer minimum stays). Amsterdam's extremely low availability (mean 92.5 days, median just 18) and minimal 30+ night requirements (1.2%) suggest a different regulatory approach — capping the total number of rental days rather than the minimum stay. Bangkok shows the highest mean availability (252.5 days), consistent with a less regulated environment.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS -->
<section id="key-takeaways">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Actionable insights for investors, hosts, and policymakers drawn directly from the data.</p>

  <ol class="takeaway-list">
    <li>
      <strong>1. Amsterdam is the pricing leader — but access is constrained.</strong>
      At $222 median nightly rate with 82.8% Entire Home share, Amsterdam delivers the highest per-listing revenue potential among cities with reliable USD pricing. However, its mean availability of just 92.5 days/year (lowest of all cities) and ultra-low professional host share (6.8%) suggest aggressive regulatory caps that limit scale opportunities for commercial operators.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>2. Bangkok and Barcelona are the most commercially operated markets.</strong>
      With 60.4% and 58.3% professional host shares respectively, these cities have the highest concentration of multi-property operators. For investors, this signals mature professional infrastructure but also intense competition and potential regulatory backlash. Barcelona's 40.1% rate of 30+ minimum-night requirements shows that regulatory response is already underway.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>3. Rome punches above its weight in guest demand.</strong>
      Rome's 1.77 average reviews/month and 14.35 average LTM reviews — both the highest of any city — indicate exceptionally strong booking velocity. Combined with moderate pricing ($131 median), moderate professional share (30.9%), and high availability (218.6 days), Rome may offer the best risk-adjusted opportunity for market entry.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>4. London is the scale play with moderate economics.</strong>
      The largest market by far (16,674 listings), London has moderate pricing ($135 median), moderate professional share (32.2%), and middling demand metrics. Its sheer volume makes it the market for operators seeking portfolio diversification, though per-unit economics are unremarkable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>5. Paris regulation is reshaping supply — pricing data is a blind spot.</strong>
      The complete absence of price data for Paris's 13,927 listings is itself a significant finding — the dataset cannot support pricing decisions for the world's most-visited city. What the data does show: 88.2% Entire Home share (highest), a mean minimum-night requirement of 43.9 (highest), and 20.4% of listings requiring 30+ nights. Paris's regulatory environment is visibly pushing the market toward longer stays.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>6. Data quality limits cross-market conclusions.</strong>
      Three of the originally specified 10 cities (New York, Tokyo, Los Angeles) are entirely absent. Two cities (Paris, Sydney) have no pricing data. Bangkok's prices are in local currency. These gaps mean this analysis covers at most 5 cities with fully comparable pricing data. Any investment or regulatory benchmarking should supplement these findings with primary data collection for the missing markets.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- METHODOLOGY -->
<section id="methodology" style="background:#f1f3f5;">
  <h2 style="border-left-color:#888;">Methodology & Data Notes</h2>
  <p style="font-size:0.9rem; color:#555; margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Data source:</strong> Inside Airbnb (insideairbnb.com), scraped 2026-03-29. Total dataset: 50,000 listings across 7 cities.<br>
    <strong>Professional host definition:</strong> Hosts with <code>calculated_host_listings_count &ge; 5</code>. This is a listing-level metric (share of listings controlled by professional hosts), not a host-level count.<br>
    <strong>Price handling:</strong> All price statistics computed on non-null values only. No imputation applied. Cities with &gt;99% missing prices (Paris, Sydney) are excluded from pricing analyses. Bangkok's prices appear to be in Thai Baht and are reported separately.<br>
    <strong>Demand proxies:</strong> <code>reviews_per_month</code> computed on listings with at least one review. Listings with zero reviews are excluded from average reviews/month but included in "% with reviews" calculation.<br>
    <strong>Missing cities:</strong> The original brief specified 10 cities (adding New York, Tokyo, Los Angeles). These cities are not present in the dataset provided.
  </p>
</section>

</div>

<div class="footer">
  Global Airbnb Market Comparison Report &middot; Generated April 2026 &middot; Data: Inside Airbnb (CC BY 4.0)
</div>

</body>
</html>"""

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"Report generated: /app/output/final_report.html")
print(f"File size: {len(html):,} bytes")

